  <h2>Acceptance of these terms</h2>
  <p>By using this website, you agree to these terms. If you don't agree with them, please don't use the site.</p>

  <h2>Purpose of this site</h2>
  <p>The Theory of Economic Relativity ("TER") website publishes an open economic research framework: its theory, executable replication tests, documentation, and related materials. Everything here is provided for research, educational, informational, and scientific purposes.</p>

  <h2>Descriptive, not normative</h2>
  <p>TER is descriptive and analytical: it represents how agents pursue objectives, perceive feasible actions, and select among them, and how those decisions produce outcomes. It does not prescribe or recommend particular economic policies, business strategies, or personal decisions.</p>

  <h2>Not professional advice</h2>
  <p>Nothing on this site — the theory, the executable tests, simulations, models, examples, or any other research output — constitutes legal, financial, investment, tax, or other professional advice. Consult a qualified professional before making decisions based on any of it.</p>

  <h2>No guarantee of accuracy or fitness for a purpose</h2>
  <p>TER is a research framework under active development. There is no guarantee that the theory, its tests, simulations, models, examples, or other research outputs are complete, error-free, predictive of real-world outcomes, or suitable for any particular decision. Replication tests demonstrate whether the framework can represent a given economic mechanism under stated assumptions — they are not proof that the framework is correct or complete.</p>

  <h2>Research and testing nature of the framework</h2>
  <p>TER, including its executable framework and replication test suite, is public research under active development and testing. Its scope, definitions, and test coverage are expected to change over time as the project continues.</p>

